@@ -153,3 +153,21 @@ Every arm PR was a draft and is closed unmerged; the `probe/1688-*` branches sta
 What `prHourlyLimit: 2` still costs, stated so it is not mistaken for a fix: in a week when both grouped branches refill, the Monday run opens lock-file maintenance and one grouped PR, and the other grouped PR and any new major wait. The hourly count resets each clock hour, so a second in-window run would open them the same day; but the 12:00Z cron almost always starts after the window closes (17:51Z to 21:12Z, measured 10-21), so in practice they usually wait for the next Monday with a free slot. Raising the limit was option (c), not chosen.
 
 `validate.yml` now refuses three things. Under `not-pending`, an unscoped rule must set `prCreation: immediate` for `major`, and no later rule reaching `major` (a rule with no `matchUpdateTypes` counts) may set anything else. `prCreation: immediate` must not reach `digest`, whether top-level, in a `digest` block, or by a rule naming `digest` or naming no update type. And with lock-file maintenance enabled, the last `prPriority` rule reaching it (a typeless one counts) must be unscoped, match `lockFileMaintenance` alone, and give it the strictly highest `prPriority`. Nine mutations turned the step red, each with its own error: the majors rule removed, scoped, or overridden by a later scoped `not-pending` rule; `digest` added to it; the priority rule removed, set to 0, outranked, scoped, or moved inside `lockFileMaintenance` (where Renovate does not accept it). The unchanged patched preset passed, and `main`'s preset at `a726791` failed on the majors guard. Review round 1 (session model) found the guards too narrow (a priority rule that also matched minor and patch tied with the groups and passed; a typeless `prCreation` override and three other routes to an immediate digest passed), and found the first text calling the next-Monday wait certain when it is only usual; both were fixed before this landed, with six more mutations. Round 2 found one more: a later typeless or scoped `prPriority: -1` tied lock-file maintenance with the groups again and passed; that and a false refusal of a harmless later scoped `immediate` major rule were fixed too, with the mutations listed in reddoor-maintenance#1712.
+
+## 2026-11-02 — The staging flow drops the hourly PR limit, because `prPriority` does not reach across base branches (patch from reddoorla/reddoor-maintenance, P1-80b)
+
+**Why.** On Monday 11-02 the in-window runs on revogen (41389005679, started 8:32pm PT Sunday 11-01, 04:32Z Monday) and medical-solutions-of-texas (41392924248, 9:15pm PT Sunday, 05:15Z Monday) each created exactly two PRs, both into `main`: `main-all-minor-patch` and `main-held-minor-patch`. All five `staging` branches on each (lock-file maintenance, the actions/setup-node digest, Node 26, @prismicio/svelte 3, svelte-select 6) were listed Rate-Limited on the Dependency Dashboards (revogen#53, medical-solutions-of-texas#50) and got no branch. Under the Monday-only schedule a branch without a PR can be created only in the one in-window run a week, so Renovate's default `prHourlyLimit` of 2 is in effect two PRs a week per repo, and the `main` groups take both.
+
+**Belief corrected: "lock-file maintenance takes the first hourly PR slot" (the 2026-10-24 entry above).** It does on a one-base repo, where it was proven (reddoor-starter#208). It does not on the staging flow. Read from Renovate 44.199.2, the version that ran: `sortBranches`, which applies `prPriority`, is called at the end of `lookup()` in lib/workers/repository/process/extract-update.ts, once per base branch, and lib/workers/repository/process/index.ts concatenates each base's sorted list in `baseBranchPatterns` order. `writeUpdates` then walks that list as given. So every `main` branch is processed before every `staging` branch, and no `prPriority` value can move a `staging` branch ahead. The 10-24 note in `renovate-config.json` is left as written, with a dated CORRECTION entry after it.
+
+**The change.** `renovate-staging-flow.json` sets `prHourlyLimit: 0`, which reaches only the repos that extend it: fifteen on 2026-11-02 (1836dig, 29-navy, alamo-anatomy, beachfront-dentistry, caltex-landing, data-dynamiq, espada, gallerysonder, la-homelessness-initiative, la-homelessness-youth, mantis-landscaping, medical-solutions-of-texas, revogen, roalson-interests, vida-legacy-foundation), every one with the same two-line `extends` and no limit of its own. In lib/workers/global/limits.ts, `calcLimit` returns 0 when any upgrade's limit is 0, and the hourly check runs only when the limit is non-zero. Called directly from the installed renovate@44.199.2 with the hour's count at 2, `isLimitReached('Branches')` returned `true` for a limit of 2 and `false` for 0. With 10 branches already existing it returned `true` again: the check there is `branchConcurrentLimit`, which is null and so inherits `prConcurrentLimit` (default 10), counted over both bases; `prConcurrentLimit` itself applies again at PR creation. The limit decides when a PR opens, not whether it may merge: majors still wait for a human, and `staging` lock-file maintenance automerges exactly as it did in a week when it won a slot.
+
+**Rejected:** putting `staging` first in `baseBranchPatterns` (it starves the `main` groups instead, and validate.yml pins the order); a finite higher limit such as 4 (two `main` groups plus five `staging` branches is already seven on these two repos, and any cap re-creates the same ordering problem one slot later); a `prHourlyLimit` packageRule scoped to `staging` (in `calcLimit` a non-zero rule caps the branches it matches, and it is harder to read than one top-level key).
+
+**validate.yml** now refuses a staging-flow preset whose `prHourlyLimit` is not exactly 0, and any packageRule in either preset that sets `prHourlyLimit` (one in renovate-config would reach the staging-flow repos too; round 1 found that gap). Six mutations each failed the step: the key removed, set to 2, set to the string "0", set to null, and a packageRule with 2 or with 0. `calcLimit` treats null as no limit and a packageRule with 0 changes nothing; the guard refuses both anyway so the rule stays one readable key. The patched preset passed; `main`'s at 1a6e3b7 failed. `renovate-config-validator --strict` (44.199.2) passed both patched files and refused a copy carrying `nonsenseOption`.
+
+**Four of the five, not five.** Review round 1 (Opus) found the first draft promising all five branches. The hourly check comes before the stability check in the branch worker (lib/workers/repository/update/branch/index.ts, 192 then 358), so the actions/setup-node digest, which has no release timestamp, stops being rate-limited and becomes pending: `Skipping branch creation due to internal status checks not met`, as renovate-config's 10-24 note already says for digests. Lock-file maintenance, Node 26 and the two majors open, unless a run stops early: `writeUpdates` stops processing branches after an automerge, and the job restarts only once, so a run that merges a `main` group and then a carried-over `staging` PR leaves the branches after it for the next window. That gate predates this change and is rare, because out-of-window runs usually merge those PRs first (review round 2).
+
+**Expect a wave.** The first in-window run after this lands opens every staging-flow repo's waiting `staging` branches at once, up to 10 per repo, mostly majors that wait for a human.
+
+Not proven live. The first in-window run after this lands (a Monday before 6pm UTC) is the check: on each staging-flow repo, the `staging` branches other than digests should open in the same run as the `main` groups.
